    <!-- How it works -->
    <string name="how_shizuku_title">Shizuku</string>
    <string name="how_shizuku_body">Android does not let normal apps see other apps\' processes or privileged settings. Shizuku runs a small server with the rights of the adb shell (or root, if you use Sui) and lends them to apps you approve.</string>
    <string name="how_helper_title">The helper process</string>
    <string name="how_helper_body">Picket asks Shizuku to start a helper, \"app.brokoli5191.picket:privileged\", which runs with those rights. It stops when Picket stops and only answers Picket.</string>
    <string name="how_typed_title">No shell, only fixed operations</string>
    <string name="how_typed_body">The helper cannot run arbitrary commands. It knows a fixed list of operations, checks every package and component name it receives, and runs each command as a list of arguments, never through a shell. Before any change you see the exact command line.</string>
    <string name="how_offline_title">No internet permission</string>
    <string name="how_offline_body">A tool with shell rights should not be able to send data anywhere. Picket does not request the internet permission, and its build fails if anything tries to add it.</string>
    <string name="how_reboot_title">After a reboot</string>
    <string name="how_reboot_body">Shizuku does not survive a restart. Start it again from the Shizuku app; with wireless debugging you may need to pair again.</string>
    <string name="how_restricted_title">Restricted settings (Android 13+)</string>
    <string name="how_restricted_body">Android blocks sideloaded apps from getting notification or accessibility access through Settings. When Picket undoes a revocation it restores that access through the shell, which works even then.</string>
